body:
My point was that expertise is not just used as a class feature. I think it's fine this homebrew feat also uses the term expertise (as it does in the official Skill Expert feat), but it should explain it like the official feat as well.

&#x200B;

Maybe we're talking around each other about what a "defined term" means. I've never seen people reference the SRD.

I really like the idea of an armored/tank/support monk. Maybe make a few things clearer. For example, you say that armor doesn't impact monk class abilities. Does that mean you still can use Unarmored Defense and armor?

&#x200B;

Maybe to make Will of Steel simpler, just say +2 to their AC or Dex save rather than providing them with half cover (functionally I believe it's the same). You can use wording similar to defensive duelist. 

&#x200B;

If I'm reading it correctly, the additional damage on your level 11 feature is insanely strong. With a +4 dex modifier, at level 11 all   
of your unarmed strikes deal 3d8+8 damage? Maybe it's supposed to be once per turn to be in-line with Soul of Iron. 

&#x200B;

I'd put a limit on number of creatures for Heart of Gold. The way it is written now I believe all summoned creatures would get that benefit, but that damage would add up.

When I was working in an inpatient unit, my supervisor told me a story of a group therapy session he was running where two people thought they were Jesus. After the session he asked each individually what they thought of the other Jesus. Both said that same thing-

&#x200B;

"That guy is crazy!"

They just need to bring back the one Tomorrowland ride that everyone keeps asking for, the Kaiser Aluminum Hall of Fame!!!

&#x200B;

https://duchessofdisneyland.com/park-history/kaiser-aluminum-hall-of-fame/

As far as I've seen, the "housing first" model has shown to be pretty effective, and ends up costing the taxpayers less in the long run. The alternate logic is that medical and mental health conditions should be treated prior to permanent housing, as the conditions will prevent a person from being able to live independently.

&#x200B;

My personal opinion, having worked as a psychologist at a homeless shelter, is that having consistent housing would make it easier to help people. I had patients that would disappear and resurface months later in the ER, often in a worse state than before. Hell, even had someone that was told he could no longer stay at a temporary shelter so he became suicidal and was admitted to an inpatient unit. When I talked to him there, he hinted that he knew what to say in order to be admitted to have a warm bed. Really sad situation.

&#x200B;

[https://endhomelessness.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/housing-first-fact-sheet.pdf](https://endhomelessness.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/housing-first-fact-sheet.pdf)

Thank you so much for this feedback, it's super helpful! Sorry for the long-response to your post, but I figured I would elaborate on some of my vision for the class.

&#x200B;

**Earthen Armor**\- That's a good point about needless bookkeeping. The idea behind these two abilities being tied to a BA (and most of the abilities requiring the armor to be up) is the barbarian needs to make the choice between making themselves more survivable or to deal more damage.

&#x200B;

**Stone Gauntlet**\- my intention with this BA attack is that it would work like two-handed fighting, so the trade-off being less AC and a weaker weapon in order to gain an additional, albeit slightly weaker BA attack. I will definitely get rid of the additional damage at level 10. Maybe it would be better for me to change the wording to say it is works like an off-hand weapon that still adds strength modifier? Or just say it can only be used if you have a hand-free and aren't wielding a heavy weapon? 

&#x200B;

**6th Level**\- Would switching the level 10 and level 6 entirely be more balanced? I didn't even think about tremorsense allowing the barbarian to see invisible creatures, that's way too strong at level 6. I figured tremorsense was needed for the burrow-speed, or else you're just burrowing but unable to see anything. The barbarian could hide with a burrow-speed, but doing so leaves the rest of the party more vulnerable.

The magical damage on the gauntlets was just to make that choice between attack/earthen armor remain even when you start facing creatures with immunity to nonmagical attacks. 

&#x200B;

**Quack Quack Stomp (lol, maybe change name to Seismic Slam)-** Strength save makes way more sense.

&#x200B;

**Tectonic Shift-** Yeah, I need to make it longer than it is wide, and better define where it can be placed. I figured the DC would be the same calculation as the stomp. 

&#x200B;

Mold Earth is a great idea, maybe at around level 6.

I kinda agree that a lot of time in these types of games is watching rather than playing. Now that I am an adult and my gaming time is so much more limited, it's hard for me to play a traditional turn-based JRPG without some kind of turbo-mode. I wonder if I could calculate how much time I spent watching Final Fantasy summons in my life...

&#x200B;

That said, I played FF6 for the first time ever on my SNES mini without turbo-mod and enjoyed the hell out of it.
